\title{HAFT: Adversarial Robustness Benchmarking and Budgeted Adaptive Auditing for Hindi Automated Fact-Checking}

\author{Anonymous NAACL Submission \\
  Track: Fact-Checking, Adversarial NLP, and Multilinguality \\
  Paper under Double-Blind Review}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Automated fact-checking (AFC) in Hindi---one of the world's most widely spoken languages, yet critically under-resourced in NLP safety benchmarks---is acutely vulnerable to disinformation. We introduce \textbf{HAFT}, an adversarial benchmark spanning 1,120 Hindi claims across six civic domains and 22 attacks (24,640 evaluations; 18,304 over clean claims) with automated quality gating. Verifiers exhibit evidence-overwrite failure under evidence injection (Threat Model B), flipping 41.0--47.7\% of claims, with 96.33\% of audited evidence failures (578/600) reproducing on Llama 3 70B. Character perturbations achieve under 3.5\% ASR on the evaluated decoder verifier, consistent with subword robustness. Under budgeted auditing ($K \le 5$), static top-5 rankings exploit dominant evidence attacks but leave systematic long-tail blind spots: 34 identified claims (4.09\%) resist all top-five attacks; excluding word jumbling leaves 20 claims (2.40\%), with 15 (1.80\%) collapsing strictly under surface edits. The \textbf{Hybrid Warm-Start Auditor} achieves 83.71\% $\pm$ 2.66\% discovery (+17.49 pp over warm-start random; 37.0 rescues per split). Crucially, unconstrained RL yields 0\% recall across 35 seed-level blind-spot evaluation instances, whereas a coverage-aware policy recovers 51.43\% (18/35) while matching Static Top-5's 86.71\% average discovery, demonstrating that average-case reward optimization alone does not ensure vulnerability coverage.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Automated fact-checking (AFC) aims to assess the veracity of factual assertions against retrieved evidence documents \citep{thorne2018fever,schuster2021vitaminc}. While multilingual corpora such as X-Fact \citep{gupta2021xfact} and regional benchmarks \citep{kumar2022hindi} have expanded evaluation to Indic languages, existing testbeds evaluate classification accuracy solely on clean, unperturbed text. Recent investigations reveal that modern NLP pipelines fail catastrophically when adversaries perturb claims, manipulate retrieved documents, or corrupt evidence alignment \citep{wang2021textflint,liu2025adversarial}. Despite these acute vulnerabilities, to the best of our knowledge, no existing benchmark provides an Indic adversarial testbed, a controlled taxonomy across linguistic levels, reproducible quality gating, and sequential budgeted attack selection.

HAFT addresses this gap by formalizing a 22-attack quality-controlled benchmark over 1,120 real-world Hindi claims and evaluating budgeted defensive auditing. In low-resource public-interest auditing, defensive red-teaming operates under strict query budgets: a fact-checking organization verifying streaming claims cannot afford brute-force evaluation across dozens of attack tools per claim against commercial or rate-limited LLM verification endpoints. However, attack selection that maximizes average vulnerability discovery does not necessarily maximize vulnerability coverage: a fixed global ranking that repeatedly fires high-ASR attacks achieves high average-case yield, but systematically under-covers claim-specific vulnerabilities in the long tail. Budgeted auditing therefore requires balancing average yield with adaptive, coverage-aware exploration.


HAFT investigates three foundational research questions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{RQ1 (Vulnerability Measurement).} Which adversarial mechanisms cause reliable failures in Hindi automated fact-checking, and how do frontier LLMs perceive Hindi vulnerability?
\item \textbf{RQ2 (Quality Gating and Cross-Model Consensus).} Do measured vulnerabilities reflect cross-architecture vulnerabilities or model-family artifacts of a specific verifier?
\item \textbf{RQ3 (Budgeted Defensive Auditing).} How can an auditor discover claim vulnerabilities under a constrained verifier query budget ($K \le 5$ of 22 attempts), and how does adaptive exploration compare with fixed empirical attack rankings?
\end{itemize}

Our principal empirical contributions are:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{A measured Hindi adversarial benchmark}: 1,120 claims across 6 sensitive civic domains, 22 controlled attacks, and 24,640 total verifier evaluations (18,304 evaluations across the 832 verified clean-correct claims) with authentic journalistic evidence across 144 primary publisher sources.
\item \textbf{Asymmetric vulnerability and frontier LLM disconnect}: Evidence-context manipulation (41.0--47.7\% unconditional ASR) is substantially more damaging than character perturbations ($<$3.5\% unconditional ASR). GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, and Kimi-k1.5 achieve only 27.27\% accuracy and 0\% recall on NEG attacks in zero-shot attack-tier prediction.
\item \textbf{Empirical identification of non-top vulnerabilities}: Static top-5 empirical rankings suffer from blind spots: 34 unique claims (4.09\% of clean baseline claims) are completely immune to all top-5 empirical arms, but collapse under non-top perturbations; excluding word jumbling leaves 20 unique claims (2.40\%), of which 15 claims (1.80\%) collapse strictly under fine-grained surface character, phonetic, and typo edits (excluding syntactic omission), significantly concentrated in Crime \& Public Safety (Bonferroni $p = 0.0402$).
\item \textbf{The Hybrid Warm-Start Auditor and Diagnostic Evaluation}: A sequential policy combining empirical priors (Steps 1--2) with reinforcement learning adaptation (Steps 3--5), achieving 83.71\% $\pm$ 2.66\% vulnerability discovery (+17.49 pp over warm-start random; 95\% bootstrap CI $[{+}14.97, {+}20.00]$ pp; 37.0 rescues per split). Static Top-5 serves as an offline benchmark for average-case discovery rather than an adaptive deployment policy, since its attack distribution is fixed globally and does not condition on claim-level audit outcomes. Our primary finding is not that reinforcement learning outperforms a fixed attack ranking, but that attack-selection objectives optimized for average discovery systematically under-cover claim-specific vulnerabilities (yielding 0\% recall across 35 seed-level blind-spot evaluation instances, compared to 22.86\% under random exploration), whereas a coverage-aware policy recovers 51.43\% (18/35).
\item \textbf{Cross-architecture consensus and verifier diagnosis}: We reduce evaluator subjectivity through a pure-Python deterministic gate, accompanied by an independent 966-query audit on Meta Llama 3 70B confirming 73.19\% overall transfer (and 96.33\% across the 600-pair audited evidence-manipulation subset spanning Threat Models B and C: AdvAdd, Fact2Fiction, ContextReplace), diagnosing Fact Mixing as a verifier-specific sensitivity, and an evaluation across decoder and encoder architectures (\texttt{mDeBERTa-v3}, \texttt{IndicBERTv2}) revealing contrasting failure modes.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\textbf{Automated fact-checking.} Fact verification benchmarks originated in English with FEVER \citep{thorne2018fever} and LIAR \citep{wang2017liar}. \citet{schuster2021vitaminc} introduced VitaminC, formalizing contrastive evidence where factual modifications flip verifier predictions. Multilingual benchmarks such as X-Fact \citep{gupta2021xfact} and Hindi fake-news corpora \citep{kumar2022hindi} evaluate veracity classification on clean text, leaving adversarial vulnerability in Indic languages unmeasured. Pre-trained Indic encoders such as IndicBERT \citep{kakwani2020indicnlpsuite,doddapaneni2023indic} provide foundational multilingual representations.

\textbf{Knowledge conflicts and RAG evidence poisoning.} In retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and automated verification, knowledge conflicts arise when external retrieved contexts contradict internal parametric knowledge stored in LLM weights \citep{xie2024adaptive,xu2024clasheval,zou2024poisonedrag}. While English RAG investigations demonstrate that frontier LLMs often vacillate between parametric memory and contextual evidence, the dynamics in low-resource Indic languages remain largely unexamined. In Indic automated fact-checking, where parametric knowledge of regional and vernacular entities is sparse, verifiers do not reliably express uncertainty when authentic and injected evidence conflict: rather than flagging unverified claims as conflicting or abstaining with an indeterminate verdict (NEI), verifiers uncritically defer to injected evidence, producing near-total verdict overwrite.

\textbf{Adversarial attacks on fact-checking.} \citet{liu2025adversarial} and \citet{wang2021textflint} survey adversarial fact-checking and NLP robustness. Evidence-poisoning techniques include AdvAdd \citep{du2022synthetic}, Fact2Fiction \citep{fact2fiction2026}, Fact-Saboteurs \citep{abdelnabi23usenix}, and evidence omission \citep{atanasova2022fact}. HAFT adapts these mechanisms to Hindi Devanagari alongside 14 deterministic rule-based algorithms under uniform quality control.

\textbf{Reinforcement learning for selective auditing.} Policy optimization has been applied to select knowledge subgraphs \citep{zhang2024akafake} and filter training samples \citep{yang2024reinforcement}. In contrast, HAFT deploys offline policy optimization \citep{williams1992reinforce,sutton2000policy} to sequentially select adversarial attack mechanisms under a constrained testing budget.

\section{The HAFT Benchmark and Threat Model}
\label{sec:benchmark}

\subsection{Task and Threat Models}
Each benchmark instance is a tuple $x_i = (c_i, e_i, y_i)$, comprising a Hindi claim $c_i$, evidence text $e_i$, and ground-truth stance $y_i \in \{\mathrm{SUP}, \mathrm{REF}, \mathrm{NEI}\}$ (Supported, Refuted, Not Enough Info). The clean verifier predicts $\hat{y}_i = V(c_i, e_i)$. Throughout our 24,640 benchmark evaluations, the primary verifier $V$ is \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} (OpenAI, temperature 0.0, top\_p 1.0, deterministic greedy decoding), selected for its strong instruction following and multilingual capabilities. Independent transfer verification is audited on open-weight \texttt{Meta-Llama-3-70B-Instruct} (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}). An attack generates perturbed text $(c'_{ij}, e'_{ij}) = A_j(c_i, e_i)$, yielding attacked verdict $\hat{y}_{ij} = V(c'_{ij}, e'_{ij})$. A raw flip occurs when $\hat{y}_{ij} \neq \hat{y}_i$.

Our attack taxonomy originated from an initial comprehensive catalogue of 53 candidate adversarial mechanisms across Devanagari morphology, syntax, semantics, and evidence tampering. To ensure rigorous, uniform evaluation within feasible computational budgets, we prioritized 22 representative attack mechanisms spanning all threat models and linguistic granularities for exhaustive benchmarking across all 1,120 claims (24,640 total verifier evaluations; Table~\ref{tab:attacks}); the remaining 31 candidate mechanisms represent unmeasured prioritization hypotheses for future expansions (see the Limitations section).

We delineate adversary capabilities into three distinct threat models:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{Threat Model A (Claim Manipulation)}: The adversary modifies the claim string ($c'_i \neq c_i, e'_i = e_i$). This encompasses two operational paradigms: (1) \emph{Intent-Preserving Surface Invariance}, where the adversary introduces character-level orthographic noise, phonetic perturbations, typos, or synonym substitutions under strict meaning-preservation constraints; and (2) \emph{Semantic Claim Blending} (\texttt{FactMixing}), where the adversary synthetically merges the target claim with contradictory predicates from another claim, testing verifier sensitivity to composite assertions.
\item \textbf{Threat Model B (Simulated Evidence-Context Injection)}: The adversary injects externally synthesized or poisoned passages into the retrieved evidence pool ($c'_i = c_i, e'_i = [e_i \,\|\, e_{\text{poison}}]$). \textbf{Authentic evidence is retained in the prompt}. In our benchmark, the injected passage is appended to authentic reference text without explicit source attribution tags, reflecting flat-context retrieval concatenation. Under ideal verification, when authentic publisher evidence conflicts with an unverified injected passage, a robust AFC system should either flag the contradiction, prioritize authenticated provenance, or output NEI. An adversarial failure occurs when the verifier uncritically yields to the injected passage and flips a true claim to REF.
\item \textbf{Threat Model C (Direct In-Situ Evidence Tampering)}: The adversary modifies authentic evidence text in-situ ($c'_i = c_i, e'_i \neq e_i$; e.g., syntactic omission of qualifying clauses, contextual sentence replacement). In Threat Model C, the original evidence is tampered with (VitaminC-style), evaluating whether the verifier detects source manipulation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Dataset Provenance and Baseline Isolation}
The benchmark consists of exactly 1,120 claims sampled across 6 diverse domains: Health \& Medicine (189), Politics \& Elections (189), Government Schemes (189), Disaster \& Breaking News (189), Celebrity News (189), and Crime \& Public Safety (175). Label distribution: 571 SUP (51.0\%), 350 REF (31.2\%), and 199 NEI (17.8\%). 

Evidence passages reflect authentic reference texts curated from 144 primary verified sources (PIB Fact Check, BBC Hindi, Dainik Jagran, Alt News, WHO fact sheets) with live URL attribution. No publisher accounts for more than 13.9\% of the corpus (Amar Ujala 13.9\%, WHO 11.1\%, PIB 9.8\%, BBC Hindi 8.5\%). Publisher coverage spans 40 sources in Government Schemes, 37 in Celebrity News, 36 in Crime \& Public Safety, and 25 in Health \& Medicine; Disaster News and Politics \& Elections draw from 3 national wires and 8 political dailies respectively, reflecting centralized coverage in emergency and legislative reporting. To prevent label leakage, all editorial debunk banners, fact-check verdict titles, and conclusion headers (e.g., ``यह दावा गलत है'') were stripped from reference texts. NEI instances were constructed from verified journalistic reports covering the claim's topic without conclusive factual confirmation.

\textbf{Dataset Splitting and Deduplication.} For offline RL policy training and budgeted benchmarking, claims are partitioned into 60\% train (499 claims), 20\% validation (166 claims, reserved for policy early stopping and checkpoint selection), and 20\% test (167 claims) splits over the 832 verified correct claims, stratified by civic domain to maintain identical domain proportions across folds. We repeat this across five fixed seeds ($42, 43, 44, 45, 46$). All 1,120 claims were verified via MinHash deduplication (Jaccard similarity $< 0.80$ on character 4-grams) to prevent event- or topic-level duplicate leakage between train and test splits.

\textbf{Clean Baseline Isolation.} Clean verifier accuracy across all 1,120 claims is 74.29\% (832/1,120). Breaking down by gold stance reveals high accuracy on verifiable assertions: \textbf{85.46\% on SUP} (488/571) and \textbf{83.14\% on REF} (291/350), but \textbf{26.63\% on NEI} (53/199). This reflects a known inductive bias in LLMs toward forcing binary verdicts rather than abstaining \citep{atanasova2022fact}. Exactly 288 clean baseline failures are isolated into a separate denominator. An attack cannot legitimately flip a claim that the verifier fails on in clean form; benchmarking strictly over the 832 verified correct claims yields exactly $832 \times 22 = 18,304$ evaluated attack opportunities.

