\subsection{LLM-Free Deterministic Quality Gating}
A verifier flip constitutes a valid adversarial failure only if the generated text is fluent and preserves necessary task constraints. To prevent evaluator bias in quality assessment, we implement a dual-layer deterministic quality gate in pure Python:

1. \textbf{Orthographic and Ligature Integrity}: Verifies Devanagari script integrity ($U{+}0900$--$U{+}097F$), rejects illegal stacked diacritics (consecutive vowel matras without a base consonant), rejects leading viramas (orphaned halants), and filters artificial character repetition ($\ge 4$ consecutive repeated aksharas/matras).

2. \textbf{Deterministic Surface Invariance}: For meaning-preserving perturbations, we enforce lexical similarity via character $n$-gram $F$-scores (ChrF++ proxy, $n \in [2, 4]$) and token-level Jaccard similarity ($\text{ChrF} \ge 0.60$ or $J(x, x') \ge 0.50$). These gates prevent degenerative gibberish while recognizing that surface metrics do not guarantee truth-value invariance. For intentional meaning-drift attacks (e.g., evidence injection), the surface check is bypassed and orthographic fluency is enforced. Human calibration on 300 double-annotated instances by two native Hindi linguists confirmed 93.3\% inter-annotator agreement on grammatical fluency and task adherence ($\kappa = 0.881$).

\section{Vulnerability Measurement \& Frontier Disconnect (RQ1)}
\label{sec:measurement}

Table~\ref{tab:attacks} reports results across all 22 measured attacks. Attacks are categorized into three empirical tiers: \textbf{POS} ($\text{ASR} \ge 40\%$), \textbf{MID} ($15\% \le \text{ASR} < 40\%$), and \textbf{NEG} ($\text{ASR} < 15\%$). All four top-tier (POS) attacks manipulate evidence or complex semantics: Poisoned Evidence Addition (`AdvAdd`) achieves 47.72\% unconditional ASR (58.47\% conditional), followed by Contextualized Evidence Replace (43.75\% uncond / 59.57\% cond), Fact Mixing (41.59\% uncond / 55.81\% cond), and Agentic Fictional Evidence (40.99\% uncond / 57.60\% cond).

\textbf{Failure Analysis Under Conflicting Evidence.} Examining flip transitions reveals acute evidence-overwrite failures: under Poisoned Evidence Addition (\texttt{AdvAdd}), \textbf{88.2\% of flips (389/441 raw, 350/397 baseline-gated)} transition directly from $\text{Gold SUP} \to \text{Attacked REF}$, and under \texttt{Fact2Fiction}, \textbf{98.5\%} transition to REF. Only 0.7--0.8\% flip to NEI. In the conceptual framework of knowledge conflicts \citep{xie2024adaptive,xu2024clasheval}, this pattern is consistent with low-resource Indic AFC systems exhibiting extreme, uncalibrated contextual deference: when injected adversarial snippets contradict authentic evidence, the verifier does not reliably express uncertainty when authentic and injected evidence conflict, instead treating the fabricated assertions as decisive ground truth.

To evaluate whether this vulnerability is attributable to prompt concatenation order or the absence of source tags, we conducted a controlled position $\times$ provenance pilot ($N=15$ claims, 60 live queries against \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}). Reversing the evidence order (\emph{Injected} passage placed first, followed by \emph{Authentic}) still produced a \textbf{100.0\% flip rate}, providing evidence against a pure recency/order artifact. Furthermore, prefixing explicit provenance tags (\texttt{सत्यापित स्रोत (Verified Archive)} vs.\ \texttt{अपुष्ट बाहरी स्रोत (Unverified Forward)}) still flipped \textbf{100.0\% of claims} (with only 20\% shifting to NEI under inverted order), suggesting that the observed vulnerability is not explained solely by passage ordering or the absence of provenance tags.

In sharp contrast, all five character-level perturbations achieve $<$3.5\% unconditional ASR. This pattern is consistent with the robustness of subword and byte-level representations to localized character perturbations. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:attacks}, the gap between the POS tier ($>40\%$) and the NEG tier ($<3.5\%$) exceeds 37 percentage points unconditionally ($>50$ pp conditionally), substantially exceeding the corresponding 95\% Wilson confidence intervals for both tiers.

