\textbf{Frontier LLM Zero-Shot Disconnect.} We surveyed 5 frontier models across diverse geographic provenance (US: GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet; China: DeepSeek-V3, Kimi-k1.5; India: Sarvam AI) to predict attack feasibility zero-shot (Table~\ref{tab:llm_metrics}). Western and Asian frontier models (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5, Kimi) achieve only 27.27\% accuracy (6/22), with a \textbf{0.0\% recall on NEG attacks} (predicting 0 of 16 actual NEG attacks as NEG; Cohen's $\kappa \le 0.158$), consistently overestimating character typos and lexical substitutions. In contrast, DeepSeek-V3 achieves 72.73\% accuracy (Balanced Acc 87.50\%, Macro $F_1$ 70.09\%, $\kappa = 0.545$) by correctly identifying 10 of 16 NEG attacks, matching the raw accuracy of the trivial Always-NEG majority baseline (72.73\%, 16/22 attacks). Sarvam AI achieves 40.91\% accuracy (Balanced Acc 58.33\%, Macro $F_1$ 39.50\%).

\section{Budgeted Defensive Auditing (RQ3)}
\label{sec:auditing}

In practical defensive auditing, a security auditor operates under a tight verifier query budget $K \le 5$ (a 77.27\% reduction in LLM verification API calls relative to exhaustive 22-attack evaluation).

\subsection{The Rescued Blind-Spot Discovery}
A static heuristic selecting the top-5 empirical arms discovers 84.62\% (704/832) of vulnerabilities across the full clean corpus, and 86.71\% $\pm$ 1.30\% averaged across five held-out test splits. However, because evidence attacks dominate, static ranking exhibits a systematic coverage limitation: it is completely blind to non-top perturbations.

We conducted a per-claim discrepancy analysis across all 832 clean claims. While Static Top-5 discovers flips on 704 claims (84.62\%), it produces \textbf{zero flips on 128 claims}. Among these blind spots, \textbf{exactly 34 unique claims (4.09\% of clean baseline claims, 3.04\% of the 1,120 corpus)} are completely immune to all top-5 empirical arms, but collapse under linguistic and syntactic perturbations. These blind spots expose a fundamental limitation of optimizing only average vulnerability discovery: the globally strongest attacks need not provide coverage of the long tail of claim-specific vulnerabilities:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=1pt]
\item Word Jumbling rescues \textbf{19 claims}. When evidence tampering fails, permuting clause word order breaks verifier reasoning. Because word jumbling preserves token bags-of-words ($J=1.0$), lexical gates cannot detect syntax scrambling; extreme permutations degrade natural fluency.
\item \textbf{Non-Jumbling Rescues (20 unique claims)}: Even under a conservative ablation strictly excluding word jumbling, \textbf{exactly 20 unique claims (2.40\%)} collapse under non-top perturbations: Syntactic Omission (7 claims; an evidence-level attack), Character Insertion (9), Character Deletion (7), Phonetic Shifts (7), Homoglyphs (6), and Typos (6). Strictly excluding both word jumbling and syntactic omission leaves \textbf{15 unique claims (1.80\%)} that collapse solely under fine-grained surface character, phonetic, and typo edits (5 of which also collapse under word jumbling, while 10 collapse solely under surface edits and resist jumbling).
\end{itemize}
Because individual claims can be vulnerable to multiple non-top attacks, per-attack rescue instances sum to 76 across the 34 claims (mean 2.24 successful non-top attacks per claim).

Crucially, these blind spots are disproportionately concentrated in \textbf{Crime \& Public Safety}: 9 of the 34 rescued claims (26.47\%) belong to Crime, despite Crime comprising only 10.70\% (89/832) of the clean corpus (Odds Ratio = 3.23, Bonferroni $p = 0.0402 < 0.05$). This concentration is even more pronounced among fine-grained surface perturbations: Crime accounts for 8 of the 15 pure surface claims (53.33\%, Bonferroni $p = 0.00026$) and 6 of the 8 strictly surface claims excluding jumbling and omission (75.00\%, Bonferroni $p = 0.00018$). A Static Top-5 audit would fail to discover these 34 vulnerabilities within the tested five-query budget, despite their vulnerability to non-top perturbations.

Across all 832 clean claims, \textbf{738 (88.70\%)} are flipped by at least one attack while \textbf{94 (11.30\%)} remain robust. Robust Accuracy $RA_K = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \prod_{a \in \mathcal{A}_K} \mathbb{I}[ \hat{y}_{i,a} = \hat{y}_i ]$ drops monotonically: $RA_1 = 52.28\%$, $RA_2 = 37.86\%$, $RA_5 = 15.38\%$, $RA_{22} = 11.30\%$ over $N=832$. On the 34 blind-spot claims, Static Top-5 obtains \textbf{0/34 by construction} because these claims define the non-top blind-spot set; the corresponding 0\% RL recall is an empirical result of the learned policy. Since Static Top-5 rankings reflect verifier-specific empirical frequencies, they are not expected to transfer across architectures with differing vulnerability profiles (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}). HAFT therefore treats defensive auditing as a sequential coverage problem.


\subsection{The Hybrid Warm-Start Auditor}
To combine the high initial yield of empirical priors with adaptive exploration for resilient claims, we introduce the \textbf{Hybrid Warm-Start Auditor}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hybrid}
\pi_{\text{hyb}}(a_t \mid \mathbf{s}_t) = \begin{cases} 
a_{\text{ctx\_rep}}, & t = 1, \\
a_{\text{adv\_add}}, & t = 2, \\
\pi_{\theta}(a_t \mid \mathbf{s}_t, \mathbf{m}_t), & t \ge 3,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $a_{\text{ctx\_rep}}$ and $a_{\text{adv\_add}}$ denote the top two empirical evidence attacks (\texttt{EA\_CTXREP} and \texttt{EA\_ADVADD}), fired deterministically at steps $t=1$ and $t=2$, resolving 62.14\% (517/832) of claims instantly. For $t \ge 3$, if the claim remains un-flipped, the trained REINFORCE policy $\pi_{\theta}$ dynamically selects among the remaining 20 attacks conditioned on state $\mathbf{s}_t$ and tried-action mask $\mathbf{m}_t$.

The policies in Table~\ref{tab:selection} optimize different notions of audit effectiveness: Static Top-5 measures the maximum yield obtainable from a fixed empirical attack distribution, whereas Hybrid performs sequential claim-conditioned selection under the same budget.

