Table~\ref{tab:selection} reports multi-seed benchmark results. Key findings:
1. \textbf{Mean Improvement Over Warm-Start Random Exploration}: The Hybrid Auditor achieves a \textbf{+17.49 pp mean improvement} over the Top-2 + Random baseline (66.23\%$\to$83.71\%; seed bootstrap 95\% CI $[{+}14.97, {+}20.00]$ pp; claim-level bootstrap 95\% CI $[{+}13.93, {+}18.53]$ pp across all 832 claims; paired $t = 12.08$, $p = 0.00027$; Wilcoxon $p = 0.0625$; paired Cohen's $d_z = 5.40$), with strictly positive per-seed gains across all five seeds ($+14.37$ to $+20.96$ pp: Seed 42: $+20.96$, Seed 43: $+20.96$, Seed 44: $+16.17$, Seed 45: $+14.97$, Seed 46: $+14.37$). Because the five-seed Wilcoxon test has a theoretical minimum $p$-value of $0.0625$ ($2 \times 0.5^5$), the result is reported with both bootstrap confidence intervals and parametric metrics rather than an ungrounded claim of non-parametric significance.
2. \textbf{Average-Case Exploitation vs.\ Vulnerability-Surface Coverage}: Static Top-5 achieves the highest in-distribution discovery (86.71\% $\pm$ 1.30\% on test splits; 84.62\% across the full corpus) because it greedily exploits the dominant empirical attack modality (evidence poisoning). The 2.09 pp difference between test splits and the full corpus is attributable to split variance: across seeds, each 60/20/20 partition sums to exactly 704 / 832 (84.62\%), with test splits drawing slightly more vulnerable claims on average (86.71\%) while validation splits draw fewer (83.13\%). On the 34 non-top blind-spot claims, Static Top-5 obtains \textbf{0/34 by construction} because these claims define the non-top blind-spot set. Crucially, when auditing resilient claims (Steps 3--5), the Hybrid Auditor approximates the high-yield behavior of the empirical ranking while retaining claim-conditioned selection: \texttt{Fact2Fiction}, \texttt{FactMixing}, and Masked-token claim rewrite (\texttt{ClaimRewrite}) account for over 92\% of RL-handover rescues across evaluated seeds. This reveals that return-maximizing RL continues to prefer high-ASR evidence and semantic attacks on resilient claims rather than autonomously exploring the long tail of fine-grained perturbations. Although Hybrid does not match the offline Static Top-5 discovery rate (83.71\% $\pm$ 2.66\% vs.\ 86.71\% $\pm$ 1.30\%; paired $t = -3.74, p = 0.020$), this gap reflects an average-discovery trade-off between maximizing average-case yield and allocating queries adaptively to claims that remain unresolved. Unconstrained RL optimized for immediate flip reward similarly failed to explore the long tail, achieving \textbf{0\% recall across 35 seed-level blind-spot evaluation instances} spanning the 34 unique claims (vs.\ 22.86\% for uniform random exploration; Fisher's exact $p = 0.005$).

\textbf{Average Discovery vs.\ Vulnerability Coverage}: When attack selection is instead optimized for blind-spot coverage on the training split, the resulting greedy coverage policy recovers \textbf{18/35 (51.43\%) seed-level blind-spot evaluation instances} on held-out test folds, while identically matching Static Top-5's average test discovery (86.71\% $\pm$ 1.30\%). Held-out blind-spot coverage across the five evaluation folds was 44.44\% (4/9), 85.71\% (6/7), 0.00\% (0/7, which selected Fact2Fiction instead of Jumbling), 57.14\% (4/7), and 80.00\% (4/5). Pooled coverage was 51.43\% (18/35); the unweighted macro mean across the five folds was 53.46\% $\pm$ 33.68\% (sample SD). These results demonstrate that attack selection that maximizes average vulnerability discovery does not necessarily maximize vulnerability coverage, and that defensive auditing requires explicit coverage-seeking exploration.
3. \textbf{Rescuing Test Claims}: Across five seeds, the RL handover rescues an average of \textbf{37.0 test claims per split} (185 total episodic rescues across runs; 42, 43, 35, 33, and 32 per seed) that withstood the top-2 evidence attacks. In terms of query efficiency, Random-5 requires 4.05 queries/claim (reaching 40.84\% at $K=5$), Top-2 + Random requires 2.65 queries/claim (66.23\% at $K=5$), Static Top-5 requires 2.48 queries/claim (86.71\% at $K=5$), and Hybrid requires 2.56 queries/claim (83.71\% at $K=5$).
4. \textbf{RL State Representation Ablation}: To test whether the policy is claim-conditioned, we ablated the state features. In an ablation isolating the learned Steps 3--5 handover under reduced epochs, masking all claim-level semantic features (the 768-dim IndicBERT claim embedding $\mathbf{z}_c$ and the 3-dim verifier verdict $\mathbf{v}$, leaving only the 88-dimensional tried-action mask and outcome history) shifts discovery from \textbf{79.84\% $\pm$ 8.32\% to 77.45\% $\pm$ 2.69\% ($-$2.39 pp, sample SD)}. Given the high fold variance ($\pm 8.32\%$), this difference is modest, indicating that while claim representations provide conditioning signal, action masking and empirical frequency priors drive the baseline policy.

\subsection{RL State, Reward, and Training Specification}
\label{sec:rl_spec}

To ensure full reproducibility, the RL attack selection MDP is formalized as follows:

\textbf{State Space ($\mathbf{s}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{859}$)}:
The state vector concatenates four distinct feature groups: $\mathbf{s}_t = [ \mathbf{z}_c \,\|\, \mathbf{v} \,\|\, \mathbf{m}_t \,\|\, \mathbf{h}_t ] \in \mathbb{R}^{859}$, where $\mathbf{z}_c \in \mathbb{R}^{768}$ is the frozen $[\text{CLS}]$ claim embedding extracted from IndicBERT v2; $\mathbf{v} \in \{0, 1\}^3$ is the one-hot encoding of the clean baseline verifier verdict $[\text{SUP}, \text{REF}, \text{NEI}]$; $\mathbf{m}_t \in \{0, 1\}^{22}$ is the binary mask of previously evaluated attacks; and $\mathbf{h}_t \in \{0, 1\}^{66}$ stores the historical 3-bit outcomes of tried attacks ($[\text{gated flip}, \text{judge pass}, \text{raw flip}]$).

\textbf{Action Space and Dynamic Masking}:
The action space $\mathcal{A}$ comprises all 22 attack mechanisms ($|\mathcal{A}| = 22$). To prevent wasteful duplicate queries, logits for previously tried attacks ($\mathbf{m}_t[i] = 1$) are masked with $-10^9$ before softmax: $\pi_\theta(a_t = i \mid \mathbf{s}_t) = \exp(\tilde{z}_i) / \sum_{j=1}^{22} \exp(\tilde{z}_j)$, where $\tilde{z}_i = z_i$ if $\mathbf{m}_t[i] = 0$ else $-10^9$.

\textbf{Reward Function and Termination}:
The reward function balances query cost against vulnerability discovery: $R_t = -0.05 + 1.0 \cdot \mathbb{I}[\text{gated flip}] + 0.5 \cdot \mathbb{I}[\text{gated flip} \land a_t \in \text{POS}]$, where $-0.05$ represents the verification API query penalty, and $+1.0$ is the flip discovery reward. The $+0.5$ term is an offline heuristic action bonus applied during training gradient updates to accelerate early policy convergence on severe evidence corruption; it is not present at test time, where the auditor executes pure argmax action selection. Thus, POS membership affects training efficiency but does not alter the test-time discovery criterion. An episode terminates immediately when a flip is found or when the budget $K=5$ is exhausted.

\textbf{Policy Architecture and Training}:
The policy network $\pi_\theta$ is a 2-layer MLP ($\text{Linear}(859, 256) {\to} \text{ReLU} {\to} \text{Linear}(256, 22)$) trained via REINFORCE with a baseline network $V_\phi$ ($\text{Linear}(859, 128) {\to} \text{ReLU} {\to} \text{Linear}(128, 1)$): $\nabla_\theta J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau} [ \sum_t \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta(a_t \mid \mathbf{s}_t) \delta_t + \beta \nabla_\theta \mathcal{H}(\pi_\theta) ]$, where $\delta_t = G_t - V_\phi(\mathbf{s}_t)$, $\gamma = 0.99$, $\beta = 0.05$. We train for 25 epochs via Adam ($\alpha_\pi = 0.001$, $\alpha_V = 0.002$), gradient clipping $\|\mathbf{g}\|_2 \le 1.0$, $\epsilon$-greedy exploration ($\epsilon = 0.10$) across five fixed seeds (42--46).

\section{Cross-Architecture Verification Consensus (RQ2)}
\label{sec:transfer}

To evaluate whether measured vulnerabilities generalize across model families or reflect artifacts of \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}, 966 completed verification pairs were audited on \texttt{Meta-Llama-3-70B-Instruct} (Table~\ref{tab:transfer}). Across all 6 audited LLM arms, \textbf{707 of 966 flips reproduced (73.19\% overall reproduction)}. For arms with $>200$ flips, 200 items were randomly sampled without replacement; for smaller arms (Claim Rewrite $n=164$, Retrieval Noise $n=2$), all available flips were evaluated.

