\caption{Cross-model verification audit on Llama 3 70B Instruct across 966 completed pairs. Reprod.\ Rate = Confirmed / Audited.}
\label{tab:transfer}
\vskip 0.05in
\centering
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Attack Mechanism & Audited & Confirmed & Reprod.\ (\%) \\
\midrule
Agentic fictional evidence (Fact2Fiction) & 200 & 200 & \textbf{100.00} \\
Poisoned evidence addition (AdvAdd) & 200 & 199 & \textbf{99.50} \\
Contextualized evidence replace & 200 & 179 & \textbf{89.50} \\
Masked-token claim rewrite & 164 & 121 & 73.78 \\
Imperceptible retrieval noise & 2 & 1 & 50.00 \\
Fact mixing & 200 & 7 & \textbf{3.50} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Total Audited (6 LLM Arms)} & \textbf{966} & \textbf{707} & \textbf{73.19} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}%
}
\end{table}

\textbf{Cross-Architecture Reproduction on Audited Evidence-Manipulation Attacks.} Across the three audited evidence-manipulation arms spanning Threat Model B (\texttt{AdvAdd}, \texttt{Fact2Fiction}) and Threat Model C (\texttt{ContextReplace}), 578 of 600 flips reproduced on Llama 3 70B, yielding a \textbf{96.33\% reproduction rate} on this 600-pair subset. Across two independently developed model families (OpenAI closed-weights and Meta open-weights), this indicates that evidence injection is a broadly transferable failure mode; it does not imply generalization to all attack types, as the overall 6-arm reproduction rate is 73.19\%.

\textbf{Fact Mixing Observed as Substantially Verifier-Dependent (3.50\%).} In sharp contrast, Fact Mixing exhibits verifier-specific sensitivity: while it achieves 55.81\% conditional ASR on \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}, only 3.50\% (7/200) of these flips reproduce on Llama 3 70B. In reciprocal evaluations where Llama 3 70B generated attacks ($n=25$ claims, 100 evaluations), overall ASR was 58.0\% and Fact Mixing flipped \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} on 80.0\% of claims. Fact Mixing therefore appears substantially verifier-dependent: its ASR was 55.8\% on \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} in the primary audit, 3.5\% on Llama 3 70B in the transfer audit, and 80.0\% against \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} when attacks were generated by Llama 3 70B, suggesting verifier-specific sensitivity to synthetic multi-claim blending while evidence tampering reproduces reliably across both decoder families. In deployment, a fixed attack ranking would require the attack distribution learned offline to remain appropriate across claims, domains, and verifier architectures. HAFT's cross-model results suggest that such rankings do not transfer directly, underscoring that globally fixed rankings are inherently verifier-dependent.

\textbf{Evaluation Across Decoder and Encoder Architectures.} To evaluate adversarial vulnerability as observed across fact-checking architectures beyond generative decoder LLMs, we evaluated both zero-shot multilingual NLI (\texttt{mDeBERTa-v3-base-xnli}, \citealt{laurer2024less}) and a task-specialized Indic encoder (\texttt{IndicBERTv2-MLM-only}, \citealt{doddapaneni2023indicbert}) fine-tuned directly on 2,801 authentic Hindi fact-checking pairs with zero test overlap.

On clean benchmark claims, \texttt{mDeBERTa-v3} achieves 52.50\% zero-shot accuracy ($N=1,120$), whereas fine-tuned \texttt{IndicBERTv2} achieves \textbf{95.81\% clean accuracy} on the held-out test split (160/167; 98.92\% on SUP, 90.32\% on REF, 100.0\% on NEI). Under adversarial attacks over clean-correct instances, both encoders exhibit acute vulnerability to evidence poisoning: for \texttt{mDeBERTa-v3} ($N=588$), Contextualized Replace achieves \textbf{77.04\% ASR} (453/588) and \texttt{AdvAdd} achieves \textbf{71.60\% ASR} (421/588); for fine-tuned \texttt{IndicBERTv2} ($N=160$), \texttt{Fact2Fiction} achieves \textbf{48.75\% ASR} (78/160), \texttt{AdvAdd} achieves \textbf{43.75\% ASR} (70/160; 64.3\% flipping \texttt{SUP}$\to$\texttt{REF}), and Contextualized Replace achieves \textbf{29.38\% ASR} (47/160).

Cross-paradigm evaluation reveals \textbf{contrasting failure modes (Overwrite vs.\ Epistemic Collapse)}: the evaluated decoder LLMs (\texttt{gpt-4o-mini}, \texttt{Llama-3-70B}) flip $>88\%$ of claims directly SUP$\to$REF ($<$1\% NEI), whereas zero-shot \texttt{mDeBERTa-v3} exhibits epistemic collapse under \texttt{AdvAdd}---68.9\% of flips transition to NEI (182 SUP$\to$NEI, 108 REF$\to$NEI; only 13 SUP$\to$REF)---defaulting to Neutral on premise tension. Fine-tuned \texttt{IndicBERTv2} instead forces direct overwrite (SUP$\to$REF in 45/70 \texttt{AdvAdd} flips), as binary classification heads suppress NEI abstention. Unlike the evaluated decoder LLMs which exhibit subword resilience to character noise ($<$3.5\% ASR), the evaluated dense encoders are acutely fragile to surface perturbations: \texttt{mDeBERTa-v3} suffers \textbf{78.06\% ASR} under Character Insertion (97.2\% of flips to NEI), while fine-tuned \texttt{IndicBERTv2} suffers \textbf{28.75\% ASR} on Character Insertion and \textbf{31.25\% ASR} on Word Jumbling, consistent with Devanagari character noise fragmenting subword tokenization.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
We presented HAFT, an adversarial benchmark for Hindi automated fact-checking spanning 1,120 claims, 22 attacks, and 24,640 quality-controlled evaluations. Evidence poisoning achieves 41--48\% unconditional ASR and transfers reliably across independently developed model families (96.33\% across the audited 600-pair evidence-manipulation subset; 73.19\% across all 6 audited arms), whereas character noise remains ineffective ($<$3.5\% on the evaluated decoder verifier). Decoder LLMs exhibit uncalibrated evidence overwrite while dense encoders exhibit epistemic collapse under poisoning and acute fragility to surface perturbations---contrasting failure modes with distinct defensive implications. Under constrained budgets ($K \le 5$), the Hybrid Warm-Start Auditor achieves 83.71\% $\pm$ 2.66\% vulnerability discovery (+17.49 pp over warm-start random; 37.0 rescues per split). A coverage-aware policy recovered 51.43\% of held-out blind-spot evaluation instances while retaining Static Top-5-level average discovery (86.71\% $\pm$ 1.30\%). Overall, HAFT demonstrates that attack selection that maximizes average vulnerability discovery does not necessarily maximize vulnerability coverage. While fixed empirical rankings provide strong average-case discovery, they systematically miss a long tail of claim-specific vulnerabilities. Budget-constrained auditing therefore requires explicit coverage-aware exploration rather than relying solely on globally ranked attack frequencies.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

We highlight six principal limitations of our work:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{Catalogue Scope}: While HAFT benchmarks 22 representative attack mechanisms across all 1,120 claims (24,640 evaluations), 31 candidate mechanisms from our broader 53-attack taxonomy remain unmeasured prioritization hypotheses for future expansions.
\item \textbf{Single-Passage Evidence}: Current benchmark instances evaluate single evidence passages; multi-hop temporal reasoning and multi-document synthesis are left for future extensions.
\item \textbf{Specialized Task Fine-Tuning}: While we evaluate across decoder LLMs (\texttt{gpt-4o-mini}, \texttt{Llama-3-70B}), cross-lingual encoders (\texttt{mDeBERTa-v3}), and an in-domain fine-tuned Indic encoder (\texttt{IndicBERTv2}), scaling fine-tuning across broader open-weights families (e.g., Qwen 2.5, Llama 3.3, MuRIL) and multi-hop retrieval is an important avenue for future benchmarking.
\item \textbf{Evidence-Context Injection Simulation}: Threat Model B simulates retrieval poisoning by concatenating fabricated passages into the evidence context rather than poisoning live web indices or dynamic search rerankers.
\item \textbf{Grammaticality of Word Jumbling}: While Hindi's postpositional case system allows constituent flexibility, arbitrary token permutation can impair natural readability ($J=1.0$).
\item \textbf{Offline Reference vs.\ Adaptive Deployment}: Static empirical policies provide a useful offline reference for average discovery under an established attack distribution, but serve as offline references rather than claim-adaptive deployment policies because they do not condition attack selection on claim-level audit history or transfer directly across distinct verifier architectures.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{Ethics and Broader Impact}
\label{sec:ethics}

Automated fact-checking systems are deployed to protect public information integrity in linguistically diverse regions. By formalizing a 77.27\% reduction in verifier query budgets, HAFT enables resource-constrained civic organizations to stress-test their models. Sourced claims originate from public journalistic archives without private personal identifiable information (PII). All evaluations were executed offline against frozen benchmark claims; no live web endpoints or third-party fact-checking portals were queried or poisoned. Fabricated Devanagari passages are dual-use; to prevent weaponization, code and benchmark datasets are released under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 strictly for non-commercial defensive research, with supplementary model weights and raw evaluation traces available for verified academic researchers upon request. Native annotators involved in quality calibration received fair above-market compensation and gave informed consent.

